Subject: [PATCH] swiftshader: raise the render target limits

SwiftShader reported MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE and MAX_RENDERBUFFER_SIZE of 8192 and
MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS of 8192, where every real Windows machine measured reports
16384, 16384 and 32767 — an NVIDIA RTX 3070 Ti, an Intel UHD 630 and even
Microsoft's own software Basic Render Driver all agree on those three, because
the numbers come from the ANGLE backend rather than the hardware.

That last clause cuts both ways, and the first version of this prose did not
admit it. 16384/16384/32767 is what D3D11 reports; it is not what a GPU is.
Measured on an M4 Max through ANGLE/Metal, the same three read 16384, 16384 and
16384x16384, and ALIASED_POINT_SIZE_RANGE ends at 511. A 32767 viewport is a
Windows value, coherent with a Windows anchor and incoherent with an Apple one.
Deciding that is the profile's job and enforcing it is the clamp's; this patch
only has to stop the driver being the thing that refuses.

The gap mattered more than it looks, because SwiftShader is the deployment
reality rather than a test artefact. An anti-detect browser runs on headless
Linux servers with no GPU far more often than not, so measuring on a machine
with a discrete card and declaring the gap closed would be assuming away the
case that actually ships.

The 8192 was never a hardware limit. It is OUTLINE_RESOLUTION, a constant sizing
the rasteriser's per-primitive span array, and MAX_FRAMEBUFFER_DIM and
MAX_VIEWPORT_DIM were both derived from it. Raising it raises a real capability
rather than reporting a false one, which is the whole distinction: patch 0026
clamps a profile's claim to what the driver can actually do, so lifting the
driver is the only honest way to let a claim of 16384 through.

Viewport is decoupled from the framebuffer because D3D11 decouples them —
32767 viewport against a 16384 framebuffer — and the span array is indexed by
framebuffer height, not viewport.

Verified, and the falsification test is the important one. A page that binary
searches the limit now finds what it was told:

  MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE      16384    a 16384x16384 texture allocates
  MAX_RENDERBUFFER_SIZE 16384    a 16384x16384 renderbuffer allocates
  MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS     32767
  32768x32768                    correctly refused

That last line is wrong and the line above it is only half right, re-measured
through the shipped 152.0.7977.83 artifact with a probe that allocates and then
attaches the result to a framebuffer, asks checkFramebufferStatus, clears to
green and reads a pixel back. SwiftShader refuses nothing: texStorage2D at
32768 and at 65536 both return no GL error, and a 3D texture eight times the
reported MAX_3D_TEXTURE_SIZE is accepted too. What fails is the framebuffer --
GL_FRAMEBUFFER_UNSUPPORTED -- and the readback, which comes back black. It fails
that way at 16384 as well, the value this patch raised the report TO, while 8192
is framebuffer-complete and reads back green. The same probe on ANGLE/Metal is
complete and green at 16384 and errors with GL_INVALID_VALUE at 32768, so on
hardware the reported number really is the usable one.

So this patch raised the reported ceiling above the usable one. That is recorded
rather than reverted, because the alternative is reporting 8192 beside a
discrete-GPU renderer string, which is a lookup-table contradiction and far
cheaper to detect than an allocation probe -- see patch 0103, which takes the
same trade deliberately and with the measurement in front of it. The competitor
failure below is still the right reference point for why the raise was made; it
is the claim that nothing is left to catch that was too strong.

This is the failure a competitor was caught by: a spoofed
MAX_VERTEX_UNIFORM_VECTORS of 4095 against a real Mesa limit of 1024, recovered
by a twelve-step binary search compiling `uniform vec4 u[N]`. Raising the real
limit moves that search out of the uniform-vector class, which is what this
patch achieves; it does not make the dimension class unfalsifiable.

Rendering is unchanged, which had to be checked: raising dimensions can silently
overflow fixed-point coordinate math, and Span packs its endpoints into unsigned
shorts. On the build host the collector's WebGL readback hashes to
918f09f66eeb4cf8 and its canvas to e6f9e629fe8af205 both before and after,
byte-identical.

Those two digests prove this change did not move the raster. They are not
evidence that software and hardware render alike, and the original wording
invited that reading. The collector's WebGL scene is an analytic mediump
gradient that every measured backend agrees on to eight bits, which is why all
four corpus anchors share 918f09f66eeb4cf8. Scenes chosen to separate them do
separate them. Rendered on one machine, switching only the backend: a
mipmapped minified quad hashes 586ce2bd… on ANGLE/Metal against b5831d63… on
SwiftShader, sliver-triangle coverage under multisampling 46a8dbcd… against
babc8e16…, a highp transcendental field 0ea5f7ac… against 85b7ec29…, and the
collector's own 2D canvas de4db56c… against 553b4533… with the same fonts
either side. Pixel divergence between the two paths is real, and nothing here
touches it.

Nor does anything here touch throughput, and nothing should. On that same
machine and binary, a fixed fragment-shader workload runs at 2517
giga-iterations per second on Metal and 9.5 on SwiftShader, and small draw
calls at 2703 thousand per second against 65 — roughly 260 and 40 times. That
gap is what makes a claimed device contradict a measured one. The answer to it
is coherence, which is patch 0091 refusing to claim a discrete GPU on a host
that has none, not a timing adjustment: deliberately shaping a render's
duration would be a new observable in its own right.

So what this patch closes is one thing precisely. The limits a page reads are
no longer numbers that no shipping configuration reports, so a profile claiming
16384 can be served instead of clamped back to 8192. It closes nothing about
how fast or how exactly the software rasteriser draws.

It also has a cost on the path where no profile serves the GPU. No compiled
anchor was captured through a software rasteriser, so patch 0093 leaves the GPU
cluster host-inherited — composing every other axis, which is its point — and
then these raised limits reach the page verbatim. Stock Chromium's SwiftShader
reports 8192/8192/8192 —
resources/fingerprints/raw/stock-linux-20260907T150627Z.json — so a software
launch with no GPU claim is enumerably a modified build. Serving a claim removes
that, because patch 0026 takes the smaller of claim and driver and
min(8192, 16384) is 8192. The remedy is therefore a swiftshader-backend anchor
in the catalogue, which is a data item and not a change to this patch.

The other cost is memory, and this is the number to count before packing a
server. Span is two unsigned shorts, so 4 bytes per entry; OUTLINE_RESOLUTION
entries per primitive, MaxBatchSize of 128 primitives per batch and MaxDrawCount
of 16 pooled draw calls give exactly 128 MiB of span arrays where there were
64 MiB. The pool is marl::PoolPolicy::Preserve, so they are constructed once and
never handed back, and RSS climbs towards the full 128 MiB as a page touches
more of it. GPU process RSS measured 221 MB on the Linux build host on a light
page; on an M4 Max it measured 357 MB under SwiftShader after a heavy WebGL
workload against 173 MB for that same page on ANGLE/Metal. None of it is
readable by a page, so it is a deployment cost rather than a fingerprint:
budget roughly 128 MB more resident memory per concurrent instance on hosts
that render in software.

Ledger: webgl.max-limits.
diff --git a/third_party/swiftshader/src/Device/Config.hpp b/third_party/swiftshader/src/Device/Config.hpp
index d030354..6303467 100644
--- a/third_party/swiftshader/src/Device/Config.hpp
+++ b/third_party/swiftshader/src/Device/Config.hpp
@@ -17,7 +17,7 @@
 
 namespace sw {
 
-constexpr int OUTLINE_RESOLUTION = 8192;  // Maximum vertical resolution of the render target
+constexpr int OUTLINE_RESOLUTION = 16384;  // Maximum vertical resolution of the render target
 constexpr int MIPMAP_LEVELS = 15;
 constexpr int MAX_CLIP_DISTANCES = 8;
 constexpr int MAX_CULL_DISTANCES = 8;
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ constexpr int MAX_TEXTURE_LOD = MIPMAP_LEVELS - 2;  // Trilinear accesses lod+1
 constexpr int MAX_COLOR_BUFFERS = 8;
 constexpr int MAX_INTERFACE_COMPONENTS = 32 * 4;  // Must be multiple of 4 for 16-byte alignment.
 constexpr int MAX_FRAMEBUFFER_DIM = OUTLINE_RESOLUTION;
-constexpr int MAX_VIEWPORT_DIM = MAX_FRAMEBUFFER_DIM;
+constexpr int MAX_VIEWPORT_DIM = 32767;  // decoupled from the framebuffer: D3D11 reports 32767 with a 16384 framebuffer, and the outline array is indexed by framebuffer height, not viewport
 
 }  // namespace sw
 
